# Writing tests: skeletons and anti-patterns

Start from the matching skeleton below. Do not invent a new setup shape when one
of these fits — copy it, then change only what your test needs.

| You are testing… | Use skeleton |
| --- | --- |
| A pure util (`parseSlides`, `mergeTextStyle`, `getEffectiveBackgroundId`) | A |
| A Zustand store action | B |
| A React component | C |
| Anything that calls `window.electronAPI` through `src/utils/ipc.js` | D |
| An Electron main-process / IPC contract | E |
| Anything with a snapshot | F |
| Time-dependent behavior (countdown, background continuity) | G |

New test files are written in **TypeScript** (`.ts` / `.tsx`). Import from the
`@` alias (`@/utils/...`), which resolves to `presenter-pro/src`.

## A — Pure util test

The cheapest and highest-value tests in this repo. No mocking, no DOM.

```ts
import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest';
import { parseSlides, parseSongGroups } from '@/utils/slideParser';

describe('parseSlides', () => {
  it('splits on blank lines and preserves newlines inside a slide', () => {
    const slides = parseSlides('line one\nline two\n\nsecond slide');
    // Exactness matters: count AND order AND content.
    expect(slides).toEqual(['line one\nline two', 'second slide']);
  });
});
```

**Derive expectations from the production constant, never a parallel copy:**

```ts
import { DEFAULT_TEXT_STYLE, mergeTextStyle } from '@/utils/textBoxes';

it('fills unset fields from the default style', () => {
  expect(mergeTextStyle({ bold: true })).toEqual({
    ...DEFAULT_TEXT_STYLE, // not a hardcoded literal that can drift
    bold: true,
  });
});
```

## B — Zustand store test

**Stores in this repo are module singletons created with `create((set) => …)`.**
There is no built-in reset, so state leaks between tests. Capture the initial
state once at import time and restore it in `beforeEach` — never rely on test
order.

```ts
import { describe, it, expect, beforeEach } from 'vitest';
import { useEditorStore } from '@/store/editorStore';

const INITIAL_STATE = useEditorStore.getState();

beforeEach(() => {
  // `true` replaces state rather than merging — a merge would leave mutations
  // from the previous test behind.
  useEditorStore.setState(INITIAL_STATE, true);
});

describe('editorStore selection', () => {
  it('selects a slide and clears any multi-selection', () => {
    useEditorStore.getState().selectSlide('sec-1', 'slide-2');
    const { selectedSlideId, selectedSlideIds } = useEditorStore.getState();
    expect(selectedSlideId).toBe('slide-2');
    expect(selectedSlideIds).toEqual(['slide-2']);
  });
});
```

Undo/redo is history-based (`HISTORY_LIMIT = 50`). When testing history, assert
the *depth* as well as the contents, so an off-by-one push doesn't pass.

## C — Component test

```tsx
// @vitest-environment jsdom
import { describe, it, expect, beforeEach, vi } from 'vitest';
import '@testing-library/jest-dom/vitest';
import { render, screen, fireEvent } from '@testing-library/react';
import { useEditorStore } from '@/store/editorStore';
import { Filmstrip } from '@/components/editor/Filmstrip';

const INITIAL_STATE = useEditorStore.getState();
beforeEach(() => useEditorStore.setState(INITIAL_STATE, true));

it('renders one thumbnail per slide in section order', () => {
  useEditorStore.setState({ presentation: presentationFixture() });
  render(<Filmstrip />);
  const thumbs = screen.getAllByTestId('filmstrip-slide');
  // Quantifier: assert the full count, not "at least one".
  expect(thumbs).toHaveLength(3);
});
```

**Read the component's gate condition before asserting a control is absent.**
The floating formatting toolbar only renders with a selected text box; media
controls depend on section type; Output Settings depends on detected displays.
A control missing because you didn't seed its precondition is a false failure.

## D — Mocking the IPC layer

`src/utils/ipc.js` is a thin wrapper over `window.electronAPI`, and every
function returns `{ success, data, error }`. **Mock that module — never import
`electron` in a renderer test.**

```ts
import { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeEach } from 'vitest';

vi.mock('@/utils/ipc', () => ({
  getPresentations: vi.fn(),
  getPresentation: vi.fn(),
  updatePresentation: vi.fn(),
}));

import { getPresentations } from '@/utils/ipc';
import { loadRecentPresentations } from '@/utils/presentationCommands';

beforeEach(() => vi.clearAllMocks());

it('surfaces the error message when the IPC call fails', async () => {
  vi.mocked(getPresentations).mockResolvedValue({
    success: false,
    error: 'db locked',
  });
  await expect(loadRecentPresentations()).resolves.toEqual({
    ok: false,
    message: 'db locked',
  });
});
```

Alternatively, stub the global directly when testing `ipc.js` itself:

```ts
beforeEach(() => {
  vi.stubGlobal('electronAPI', { getPresentations: vi.fn() });
});
```

## E — Electron main / IPC contract test

The main↔renderer seam is this app's highest-risk surface. Test the **payload
shape on both sides**, so a handler and its caller cannot drift apart.

```ts
import { describe, it, expect, vi } from 'vitest';

// Never load better-sqlite3 in a unit test — it is a native module.
vi.mock('../db/queries/presentations', () => ({
  listPresentations: vi.fn(() => [{ id: 'p1', title: 'Sunday' }]),
}));

import { handleGetPresentations } from '../index';

it('returns the { success, data } envelope the renderer expects', async () => {
  const result = await handleGetPresentations();
  // Exactness matters: the renderer destructures these exact keys.
  expect(result).toEqual({
    success: true,
    data: [{ id: 'p1', title: 'Sunday' }],
  });
});
```

If a handler is not currently exported, export it — do **not** reach into module
internals or duplicate its logic in the test.

## F — Snapshot test

Every snapshot needs a structural floor in the same test, so an empty or garbage
baseline can never pass:

```ts
it('normalizes a legacy presentation into the current shape', () => {
  const normalized = normalizePresentation(legacyFixture());
  // Floor: an empty result means the fixture or harness failed, not that the
  // presentation legitimately has no sections. Never snapshot without one.
  expect(normalized.sections.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
  expect(normalized).toMatchSnapshot();
});
```

If this snapshot ever fails, follow the triage in `testing-standards.mdc` before
touching it. Never run `vitest -u` across more than the cell you intentionally
changed, and **never combine `-u` with a `-t` filter** — a filtered update can
drop baselines for cells that didn't run.

## G — Time-dependent behavior

Countdown overlays and continuous background playback are time-driven. Use fake
timers and advance explicitly; never `await` a real delay.

```ts
import { vi, it, expect, afterEach } from 'vitest';

afterEach(() => vi.useRealTimers());

it('stops the countdown exactly at zero', () => {
  vi.useFakeTimers();
  const countdown = startCountdown({ seconds: 3 });
  vi.advanceTimersByTime(3000);
  expect(countdown.remaining).toBe(0);
  // Assert it does not go negative on the next tick.
  vi.advanceTimersByTime(1000);
  expect(countdown.remaining).toBe(0);
});
```

## Canvas and measurement

jsdom returns **zeros** from `getBoundingClientRect()`. Components that scale
from measured geometry — `Canvas.jsx`, the floating formatting toolbar,
`presentationSizing` — cannot be asserted through real layout. Extract the
computation into a pure function and test that, or stub the measurement seam and
assert on the computed output. Never assert on a pixel value jsdom produced.

## Anti-patterns (lint-enforced by `eslint-plugin-vitest`)

When one of these rules fires, **fix the test structure — do not disable the
rule**:

- `vitest/no-focused-tests` — a leftover `.only` silently disables every other
  test in the run. Remove it.
- `vitest/no-disabled-tests` — `it.skip` / `xit` is deleted coverage. Fix the
  test or fix the code; a genuinely required skip needs a line-scoped disable
  with a justifying comment.
- `vitest/expect-expect` — a test body with no assertion is permanently green.
  Add a real `expect`; if your assertions live in a helper, name it `expect*` so
  the rule recognizes it.
- `vitest/no-conditional-expect` — an `expect` inside `if` / `catch` silently
  skips. Assert the precondition first, or use a throw-narrowing helper.

Also never:

- **Assert on your own mock.** `expect(myMock()).toBe(mockReturnValue)` tests the
  mock, not the code. Assert on what the component or function *did with* the
  mock (calls, rendered output).
- **Loosen a comparison to go green** — sorting both sides, set-compares,
  `toContain` where `toEqual` failed. The order/content difference IS the signal;
  triage it.
- **Hardcode expectations that mirror production constants.** Import the constant
  (`DEFAULT_TEXT_STYLE`, `SECTION_COLORS`, `FONT_SIZE_DISPLAY_PRESETS`) so the
  test fails when reality changes rather than drifting.
- **Forget to reset a Zustand store.** A passing test that only passes when run
  first is worse than no test.
- **Load `better-sqlite3` or `electron` in a unit test.** Both are native/host
  modules; cover real database and window behavior in Playwright E2E instead.

For thrown errors, capture-then-assert — `expect` inside `catch` passes
vacuously when nothing throws:

```ts
let thrown: unknown;
try {
  parsePresentation(bad);
} catch (e) {
  thrown = e;
}
expect(thrown).toBeInstanceOf(ValidationError);
```
